# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("What's actually missing under a dipped slab",
     "<p>A slab rarely sinks because the concrete itself failed. It sinks because the compacted base underneath lost material, usually washed out by water that found a path through a joint or a crack over several rainy seasons. That missing material leaves a void, and the panel above holds its shape until enough of the void opens up that the concrete settles into the gap. Mudjacking and polyjacking both work by filling that void rather than touching the slab, which is why either method can level a surface without demolition. Figuring out how far the void actually extends under a {city} driveway, not just where the dip shows on top, decides whether injection is the right fix at all.</p>"),
    ("Why a cut-out panel needs its own base, not a patch on the old one",
     "<p>Replacing a single damaged panel means rebuilding the base under just that section, compacted to the same standard as the rest of the driveway rather than left loose because it's a smaller job. A new panel poured over soft, uncompacted fill settles again within a season or two, which defeats the point of cutting it out in the first place. On a {city} lot, that base work usually means removing whatever caused the original failure, often a wet spot that never drained, before any forms go back in. The base under one panel gets the same attention the base under a whole driveway would.</p>"),
    ("Resurfacing bonds to the surface; it doesn't touch what's underneath",
     "<p>A bonded overlay sticks to the top of an existing slab and changes nothing about the compacted base several inches below it. That's a meaningful limit: resurfacing is the right call when the base is sound and the slab's problem is cosmetic, pitting, scaling, hairline cracking, but it's the wrong call when a panel is actually settling into a void. Pouring a fresh-looking overlay over an unstable base in {city} buys a season or two of better looks before the same crack telegraphs back through the new surface. Checking what's happening below before choosing resurfacing saves redoing the job twice.</p>"),
    ("How a washed-out base shows up gradually, then all at once",
     "<p>Base erosion under an aging slab is usually slow: a small amount of sand or fines carried out through a hairline crack during each rainy-season storm, repeated over years until the void underneath is big enough to matter. A driveway can look stable for a long stretch and then develop a noticeable dip in a single wet season once the void finally collapses under traffic. On flatwoods soils common across both the Orlando and Suncoast service areas, where a seasonal high water table sits close to the surface for part of the year, that erosion process runs faster than it would on drier ground around {city}.</p>"),
    ("Why mudjacking grout and polyurethane foam don't suit the same soil",
     "<p>Mudjacking pumps a soil-cement-water slurry under a slab, and that grout has real weight once it's in place, which matters on ground that's already struggling to support the concrete above it. Polyurethane foam used in polyjacking expands into the same kind of void but weighs a fraction as much, so it doesn't add significant load to weak or saturated soil the way slurry does. On a {city} job where the base is soft or sits over flatwoods ground with a shallow water table, that weight difference is often what decides which injection method actually holds.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("What a resurfacing overlay actually consists of",
     "<p>A bonded overlay is a thin cementitious layer, typically well under an inch for a decorative finish and more for a structural resurfacing pour, applied over a bonding agent so it grips the old slab instead of sitting on top of it loosely. Pricing tracks the finish: a basic decorative overlay runs roughly $6 to $10 per square foot, while a stamped overlay with texture and color runs $7 to $20, higher for a multi-pattern design. On a {city} driveway, the existing slab has to be sound enough to bond to in the first place, since an overlay poured over an actively cracking panel just cracks along with it.</p>"),
    ("The grout mix behind mudjacking, and the foam behind polyjacking",
     "<p>Mudjacking works through small holes, usually about 1 to 2 inches across, drilled in a grid pattern, then a soil-cement-water slurry is pumped under pressure until the slab lifts back to grade. Polyjacking uses smaller holes, closer to 5/8 inch, and injects a two-part polyurethane foam that expands within the void in minutes rather than filling it with liquid grout. Mudjacking nationally runs about $4 to $9 per square foot, polyjacking about $8 to $25, a gap that largely reflects material cost rather than labor. Either method leaves a small patched hole pattern across the {city} driveway once the job is done.</p>"),
    ("Matching a cut-out panel to the slab around it",
     "<p>A replaced panel is built to the same thickness and strength as the surrounding driveway, in the 3,000 to 4,000 psi range typical of residential flatwork, with a clean saw-cut edge rather than a ragged break line. Steel dowels or tie bars set into the adjoining panels help the new concrete move with the old rather than as an independent piece that settles or heaves on its own. On a {city} job, the new panel also picks up the control-joint pattern already established around it, so the repair reads as part of the original layout rather than an obvious insert years later.</p>"),
    ("Crack filling by the linear foot, not the square foot",
     "<p>A hairline or moderate crack that isn't tied to an underlying void gets treated as a linear repair rather than a surface one: a flexible filler or sealant worked into the crack itself, priced by the foot rather than the square foot, with Orlando-area figures running roughly 50 cents to $5 per linear foot depending on width and depth. That's a different job from resurfacing the whole slab, and it's meant for a crack that isn't actively moving or widening. A {city} driveway with a handful of scattered hairline cracks but a sound, level base is usually a crack-filling job, not a resurfacing one.</p>"),
    ("Raising a slab with slurry: how far the spec goes before it's a different job",
     "<p>Concrete leveling by injection generally runs $3 to $12 per square foot, or roughly $700 to $5,000 for a typical residential job, with stone slurry grout on the higher end of that range at $6 to $15 per square foot. That pricing assumes the slab itself is sound enough to lift as one piece; a panel that's cracked into several independent sections doesn't raise evenly and usually needs cut-out-and-replace instead. Deciding which category a {city} driveway falls into, liftable as-is versus too broken to lift, is the first real spec decision in a leveling job, before any grout or foam gets ordered.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("Why the first visit is diagnosis, not a quote for one method",
     "<p>A repair job starts with figuring out why a slab cracked or sank, not with picking mudjacking, polyjacking, resurfacing or cut-out-and-replace off a list before that's known. A contractor walking a {city} driveway checks whether the ground underneath has a void, whether a crack is still moving, and whether the surrounding panels are sound enough to support an overlay or a lift. That assessment determines which method applies; a quote written before it is really just a guess dressed up as a number. Skipping this step is the single most common reason a repair doesn't hold.</p>"),
    ("A mudjacking day: holes, grout, and watching the slab rise",
     "<p>Once the void is confirmed, the crew drills a grid of small holes through the slab, typically 1 to 2 inches across, spaced to reach the full area that's settled. Grout gets pumped into each hole under pressure while someone watches the slab's edge with a level, stopping the moment the panel reaches grade rather than overshooting it. Each hole gets patched with a matching mortar once the lift is done. On a {city} job, the holes are the only visible trace of the work once the patches cure, small circles across an otherwise unchanged driveway surface.</p>"),
    ("Polyjacking moves faster per hole, but needs closer attention",
     "<p>Polyjacking's smaller injection holes and foam that expands within minutes mean a crew works through more injection points in less total time than a comparable mudjacking job, but each one needs closer monitoring since over-injecting foam can lift a slab too far or crack it from underneath. The crew checks the rise after each short injection rather than after a long, continuous pour, adjusting as they go. For a {city} homeowner comparing quotes, polyjacking's speed per hole is real, but it trades off against a process that leaves less room for a crew to work carelessly and still get away with it.</p>"),
    ("The resurfacing sequence: repair first, then the overlay",
     "<p>Resurfacing starts with pressure washing and sometimes an acid etch to open the slab's surface so the bonding agent can actually grip it, followed by patching any spalls or wide cracks before the overlay material ever goes down. The overlay itself gets applied, sometimes stamped or textured while it's still workable, then cured before a sealer finishes the job. Skipping the repair step and overlaying straight onto a dirty or flawed slab is how a {city} resurfacing job looks good on day one and shows every old flaw again within a year, right through the new surface.</p>"),
    ("Demolition, base, dowels, pour: a cut-out job in order",
     "<p>Cutting out a damaged panel starts with a full-depth saw cut around its perimeter, then breaking up and hauling off the old concrete before anyone touches the base underneath. Once the debris is clear, the crew compacts new base material, drills and sets dowels into the adjoining panels, forms the new panel, and pours it to match the original thickness and strength. Curing runs at least three days before the area sees regular traffic. On a {city} driveway, this sequence takes longer than mudjacking or polyjacking because nearly every step is visible and none of them can be skipped.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("An overlay poured on a crack that's still moving",
     "<p>Resurfacing works when the crack underneath has stopped shifting; it fails when the slab is still settling, because the new overlay bonds to a surface that keeps moving beneath it. Within a wet season or two, the same crack usually reappears in almost the same spot, right through the fresh overlay, since the overlay never addressed what caused the original movement. On a {city} job, a crack that reopens after resurfacing is less a sign of bad workmanship than a sign the diagnosis skipped the question of whether the slab was done moving before the overlay went on.</p>"),
    ("Grout that's heavier than the ground can carry",
     "<p>Mudjacking's slurry has real weight, and on soil that's already weak or saturated, usually flatwoods ground with a shallow water table, that added weight can be too much for the base to hold, so the newly lifted slab settles again within a year or two. Polyjacking's lightweight foam is often the better fit on that kind of ground precisely because it doesn't add the same load. A {city} contractor skipping a soil check before choosing between the two methods risks solving the dip today and creating the same call again next rainy season.</p>"),
    ("Over-injecting polyurethane foam past where the slab needed to go",
     "<p>Because polyurethane foam expands fast, a crew injecting without close attention can lift a slab higher than its original grade or crack it from the pressure building up underneath faster than the concrete can flex. That's a real risk of the method's own speed: the foam that makes polyjacking quick is the same property that makes it easy to overdo. On a {city} driveway, a slab lifted unevenly past level, or cracked during the lift itself, usually means replacing a section that didn't need replacing before the injection started.</p>"),
    ("A replaced panel that never quite matches the rest of the driveway",
     "<p>New concrete and old concrete age differently: the fresh panel starts out a slightly different shade, cures at its own rate, and can take years of sun exposure to weather close to the surrounding slab's color, if it ever fully matches. That's a cosmetic reality of cut-out-and-replace, not a flaw in the work itself, and it's worth setting that expectation with a {city} homeowner before the job starts rather than after. A mismatched panel that's structurally sound and correctly tied into the joint pattern is a successful repair even while it visibly reads as newer than what's around it.</p>"),
    ("When the same spot keeps failing, the question isn't the method",
     "<p>A driveway that needs mudjacking or crack filling in the same corner more than once usually isn't telling you the first repair was done wrong; it's telling you the underlying cause, often a drainage path or a persistently wet patch of ground, was never addressed. A state Senate report found more than 88 percent of Florida's sinkhole claims concentrated in eleven counties, with Orange among them and Sarasota and Manatee absent from that list, which matters for a {city} homeowner trying to tell ordinary repeat settlement from something that needs a geologist instead of another repair crew.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Watching the patched holes after a mudjacking or polyjacking job",
     "<p>The small patched circles left by injection holes are usually the only visible change to a driveway once a lift is finished, and they're worth checking periodically for cracking or a slightly sunken feel underfoot, since that can signal the void is starting to reopen rather than that the patch itself failed. If the same section needs another lift within a short span, that's a sign to look at what's feeding water to the base under that {city} driveway rather than treating the repeat injection as routine maintenance.</p>"),
    ("Caring for a fresh overlay without undoing the bond",
     "<p>A resurfaced driveway depends on the overlay staying bonded to the old slab underneath it, and an aggressive pressure wash too soon after the job, especially with a narrow tip held close to the surface, can chip or peel a thin overlay before it's fully cured. Sealing it on a schedule and avoiding harsh degreasers in the first few months protects that bond while it's still establishing. For a {city} homeowner, treating a new overlay gently for its first season is a small habit that extends how long the resurfacing actually holds up.</p>"),
    ("Giving a replaced panel its full cure before loading it",
     "<p>A new cut-out panel needs to cure, kept moist through a curing compound or wet curing, for at least three days before it sees regular use, and parking anything heavy on it before that window closes can crack a panel that would otherwise have matched the driveway's working life. On a {city} job finished in summer heat, that curing period matters even more, since hot weather pulls moisture out of fresh concrete faster than it does on a mild week. Giving the new panel its full cure is a small wait against a repair that otherwise has to be redone.</p>"),
    ("Checking a repaired spot again after the first rainy season",
     "<p>The real test of any repair, mudjacking, polyjacking, resurfacing or a replaced panel, is whether it holds through a full wet season, not whether it looks right the week it's finished. A {city} homeowner who notices the same dip or crack returning after the rains have passed through is looking at evidence the underlying cause wasn't fully addressed, not a sign the repair method itself was wrong. That follow-up check costs nothing and catches a problem early enough to fix with another targeted repair rather than a full section replacement.</p>"),
    ("Sealing hairline cracks before they become a void underneath",
     "<p>A hairline crack that's left alone keeps acting as a path for water to reach the base below, slowly carrying away the fines and sand that hold the slab up, the same process that eventually calls for mudjacking or a cut-out repair. Filling a crack while it's still small, before the next rainy season rather than after, is cheap compared with the injection or replacement work needed once a real void has formed underneath a {city} driveway. Routine crack sealing is less a cosmetic habit than a way of protecting the base nobody can see.</p>"),
  ],
}
